Report an emulated hard link as the hard link it stands in for

Patch 0003 makes link(2)/linkat(2) work under the app seccomp filter by
copying the bytes in PRoot's own process when the platform refuses a real
hard link.  A copy leaves two inodes where the guest asked for one, and
shadow(1) is what asks.  Every tool that uses a hard link as a *lock* --
adduser(8) and groupadd(8) link their database to <db>.lock and then verify
the lock by counting links -- reads the copy's st_nlink of 1 as "already
held":

    groupadd: /etc/group.2500379: lock file already used
    groupadd: cannot lock /etc/group; try again later.
    groupadd-exit=10

That is the failure the Ubuntu E2E workflow died on (run 35086244789, the
install stage), and it is reachable on a host by answering EACCES to
linkat(2)/link(2) -- the device's refusal of hard links in app data -- with a
seccomp filter around PRoot, which is how the trace above was taken.  It
fails openssh-client's postinst, so apt-get install exits 100 and the Ubuntu
install the app performs never completes.

shadow's do_lock_file() is the reader: it links /etc/group.<pid> to
/etc/group.lock, then check_link_count() requires stat().st_nlink == 2 -- the
count only a real hard link produces, and the one thing a byte copy cannot
fake.  The lock is taken by shadow, not by the kernel, so nothing about
translating paths can help; and fixing the guest instead (pre-seeding the
_ssh group) would repair this one package while leaving every other postinst
that locks a database the same way.  The emulation is what told the lie, so
the emulation answers for it.

What is recorded is only what is needed to answer: the two names, and the
identity each had when the copy was made.  A stat(2) of either name is then
answered with the group's shared inode, its device, and the number of names
in the group -- for as long as both names are still the very inodes the
emulation left behind, so a name replaced by rename(2) (dpkg rewrites its
status file that way) stops being reported the moment the pair stops being
one file.  The group is closed transitively, so link(a,b) then link(b,c) is
three names of one inode, and the inode reported is the one belonging to the
name that existed before the first emulation -- the inode a real hard link
would have kept.  Symbolic sources are not recorded at all: a symlink
recreated as a symlink is already what link(2) would have made, and no tool
asks a symlink how many links it has.

The correction runs in the exit stage rather than the enter stage, because
that is where a structure exists to correct, and on every syscall because the
guest's stat(2) of the name is a separate, later call.  It is gated on the
syscall number first and on string comparisons second, so a stat(2) of a file
with no emulated link costs nothing beyond the comparisons.  statx(2) is a
second caller rather than a second implementation: this fork's statx
emulation fills that structure itself (tracee/statx.c), so it is corrected
there, before it is written back to the guest.

The record lives in the PRoot process that made the link, and that is the
whole of its scope.  A later PRoot invocation finds an empty table and
answers with the kernel's copy -- one link each -- because the app runs one
PRoot process per command.  This is enough for what the patch is for, and
nothing here pretends to be more: shadow takes its lock and verifies it
inside a single process, and every tool with that protocol does the same, so
the question is always asked in the run that made the link.  A guest that
links in one command and stats in the next gets the truth about the copy,
which is the right answer to a question this patch never claimed to answer.
It is why the E2E probe links, stats and asks `test -ef` inside ONE session
(UbuntuE2eVerificationTest.hardLinksShareOneInode): the first version of that
probe split the three commands into three sessions and failed on run
35100526297, and the failure was the test's, not this patch's.

The correction is skipped for a 32-bit tracee.  Its struct stat64 is not the
64-bit structure this reads and writes whole, and poking it anyway would
write past the guest's buffer and corrupt its memory; abi.h carries offsets
for the two fields fake_id0 needs (uid, gid) and none for inode, device and
link count.  A 32-bit tracee therefore keeps the kernel's answer -- which is
exactly the behaviour without this patch, so nothing is lost that was ever
there.

Not upstream: it is a fidelity fix for one consequence of 0003's emulation,
and 0003 is itself not upstream.  The two should travel together if the fork
ever takes them.

Evidence, from a host build of this tree with EACCES answered to
linkat(2)/link(2) for PRoot and every tracee:

  - apt-get update (39.2 MB of package lists) followed by
    apt-get install --no-install-recommends openssh-client: exit 0.  The
    postinst runs, _ssh:x:101 appears in /etc/group and /etc/gshadow,
    /usr/bin/ssh -V prints its version, dpkg --configure -a exits 0, and no
    lock file is left behind.
  - The same groupadd -g 101 _ssh before this patch: exit 10, "cannot lock
    /etc/group; try again later." -- the A/B is a single command on one
    rootfs, and it is the exact command the postinst runs.
  - stat and ls of an emulated pair both report 2 links and the same inode,
    and test a -ef b succeeds.  link(a,b); link(b,c) reports 3 links on all
    three names.  After either name is replaced by rename(2), the survivor
    reports 1 link again.  (Every one of these ran inside a single PRoot
    process, which is the only scope the record has -- see above.)
  - On the device (E2E run 35100526297, API 35): the install phase passed for
    the first time, 304s, apt-get install exit 0 with no lock failure, where
    run 35086244789 had died in the same phase on groupadd's exit 10.  The
    install log the app records shows the dpkg repair pass exiting 0 with
    nothing to do, which is what a clean lock leaves behind.  This is what
    the patch was written for, but it is correlation, not proof: the same
    run carried the app-side dpkg repair and storage-gate changes, and
    attributing the pass to any one of them would need an A/B the E2E does
    not run.
  - A genuinely stale /etc/group.lock is still refused, with shadow's own
    "existing lock file ... with an invalid PID" and exit 10: the EEXIST is
    passed through rather than overwritten, so a byte copy can never clobber
    a lock it did not create.
